// mv-kit style kit: print.js — line-printer art. The whole frame is a strip of fanfold computer paper and
// everything on it is struck by a printer: pictures become shape-matched ASCII (with overstrike for the darks),
// words are typed in the same Courier, at 1–4× expanded print, in black or the red half of the ribbon.
//
//   const S = prSheet({ cpi: 15, lpi: 8 })      a sheet: a picture grid (any pitch) + a text grid (10 cpi / 6 lpi)
//   S.clear()                                    every frame (or per drawing, with o.key)
//   S.g …                                        Canvas 2D in sheet px (x 0..W, y 0..S.h): paint the picture.
//                                                Black = black ink, '#f00' = red ink, white = paper, greys = tone
//   S.put(col, row, 'TEXT', { red, x: 2 })       text grid: exact characters (x = expanded print, xw / xh separately)
//   S.banner('AGI', col, row, { h: 9 })          block letters made of their own characters (BANNER)
//   S.stencil(ctx => …, 'chars', o)              fill a painted shape with characters
//   S.box(c0, r0, c1, r1, { title })             +---+ frame;  S.knock(c0, r0, c1, r1) keeps the picture out
//   prPrint(g, S, { cam, reveal, key })          print it: match glyphs, then paper + ink through the camera into g
//   prPrint(g, [S1, S2], …)                      several sheets on one strip (each has its own S.oy)
//   prProject(cam, x, y) → [sx, sy, depth]       paper px → screen px (to draw over the paper in 2D)
//
// Paper coordinates: x 0..W is the 13.2" print line (132 columns at 10 cpi), the paper is 14.875" wide with the
// tractor margins outside that; y runs down the strip (11" pages, a perforation every page, a green bar every
// half inch). A sheet sits on the strip at y = S.oy. The camera {x, y, z, rot, tilt, spin} looks at paper point
// (x, y); z = 1 shows the print line edge to edge; tilt (radians) leans back so the strip recedes up the frame.
//
// Courier Prime (Regular, Bold): SIL Open Font License 1.1, kits/fonts/OFL-CourierPrime.txt, subset to ASCII.
